# 5. What "dimension expansion" now means

Suppose:

$$
\mathcal D_t
$$

contains the currently represented distinctions.

A new distinction \(D_{new}\) is discovered.

We may obtain:

$$
\mathcal D_{t+1}
=
\mathcal D_t\cup\{D_{new}\}.
$$

But the important question is **why** this is necessary.

The correct test is whether the old representation fails to preserve a newly required distinction:

$$
\exists s_1,s_2:
$$

$$
s_1\equiv_{\mathcal O_t}s_2
$$

but

$$
s_1\not\sim_{\rm req}^{Q_{t+1},\Gamma_{t+1}}s_2.
$$

Then the old representation is no longer requirement-faithful.

This is a much stronger mathematical interpretation of "discovering a new dimension."

---

# 6. Dimension expansion = possible quotient refinement

If a new required distinction is added:

$$
R_{\rm req}^{t}
\subseteq
R_{\rm req}^{t+1},
$$

then

$$
\sim_{\rm req}^{t+1}
=
\bigcap_{d\in R_{\rm req}^{t+1}}
\sim_d
$$

and therefore:

$$
\boxed{
\sim_{\rm req}^{t+1}
\subseteq
\sim_{\rm req}^{t}.
}
$$

Hence the quotient becomes potentially finer:

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal S/\sim_{\rm req}^{t}
\quad\longrightarrow\quad
\mathcal S/\sim_{\rm req}^{t+1}.
}
$$

This is, in my view, the **strongest new mathematical connection from today's research**.

We do not need to say "the vector-space dimension increased."

We can say:

> **The required observational resolution increased.**

Only later might we discover that a coordinate/dimensional representation is the most economical realization of that refinement.

