# 7. Dimension reduction

Likewise, the proposed:

$$
\mathcal D_{t+1}
=
\mathcal D_t-\{D_k\}
$$

must not automatically mean information deletion.

There are at least three possibilities:

### Removal

The distinction is no longer required.

### Compression

The distinction remains reconstructible from other represented information.

### Reclassification

Two dimensions are discovered to encode the same required distinction.

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{
\text{dimension reduction}
\neq
\text{information deletion}.
}
$$

And this connects directly with today's reduction research:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Eliminability}
\neq
\text{Preservation}
\neq
\text{Realization}.
}
$$

The experiments we reviewed today strongly support keeping those three questions separate, rather than turning a particular numerical reduction boundary into a universal law. 

