# 8. Two forms of missingness

The document's distinction here is useful and should be retained, but with different terminology.

## Representation-level missingness

$$
D_i\notin\mathcal D_t.
$$

The dimension is not represented.

## Value-level unknown

$$
D_i\in\mathcal D_t
$$

but its value is unresolved:

$$
v_i(t)=\bot.
$$

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{
D_i\notin\mathcal D_t
\neq
(D_i\in\mathcal D_t\land v_i=\bot).
}
$$

This is a legitimate structural distinction.

But we should **not yet call either one "Zero Type A/B" as a canonical Zero theory**.

Today's Zero research specifically warned us against identifying representational omission with preservation-relative Zero. The BRIDGE experiments found no tested universal relationship between Zero and preservation/adequacy. 

So use:

$$
\boxed{
MissingDimension
}
$$

and

$$
\boxed{
UnresolvedValue
}
$$

for now.

Then separately investigate whether either can be related to a Zero operator.

